Experiment 4: Fork a Repository and Create a Pull Request

Aim

To fork an existing GitHub repository, make changes, and submit a pull request to propose those changes to the original repository.

Requirements

- GitHub account
- Web browser
- Internet connection
- Git installed (optional for editing through the browser)

Procedure

1. Open the original repository on GitHub.
2. Click the Fork button to create a copy under your account.
3. Open the forked repository.
4. Create a new branch.
5. Add or modify a file.
6. Commit the changes to the branch.
7. Click Contribute and select Open pull request, or open the Pull requests tab and select New pull request.
8. Add a title and description.
9. Submit the pull request and verify that it appears in the original repository.

Example Repository

Original repository:
https://github.com/firstcontributions/first-contributions

Your GitHub account:
https://github.com/Sudiksha784

Steps Using Git Commands

After forking the repository on GitHub, clone your fork. Replace "YOUR-FORK-REPOSITORY" with the actual repository name under your account.

git clone https://github.com/Sudiksha784/YOUR-FORK-REPOSITORY.git
cd YOUR-FORK-REPOSITORY
git switch -c my-change
echo "My contribution" > contribution.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Add my contribution"
git push -u origin my-change

Then open your fork on GitHub and create a pull request targeting the original repository.

Explanation of Commands

- "git clone URL" – Downloads a repository.
- "cd REPOSITORY" – Opens the project folder.
- "git switch -c my-change" – Creates and switches to a new branch.
- "echo "My contribution" > contribution.txt" – Creates a file.
- "git add ." – Stages the changes.
- "git commit -m "Add my contribution"" – Saves the changes.
- "git push -u origin my-change" – Uploads the branch to your fork.

Expected Output

A forked repository is created, and a pull request is submitted to the original repository.

Result

Thus, a repository was forked, modified, and a pull request was created successfully.

Important Notes

- A fork is your own copy of another user's repository.
- A pull request (PR) proposes changes for the original repository to review and potentially merge.
- Creating a pull request does not automatically mean the changes have been merged.
- If using the browser, you can edit a file directly on GitHub and create a branch without running the commands above.